          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 4:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " In the equations shown below, a and b are unknown numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.427,
            0.149,
            0.576,
            0.188
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{r}3ax + 4y = - 2, \\\\2x + by = 14.\\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.192,
            0.884,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If (–3, 4) is the solution of both equations, find the values of a and b."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.237,
            0.265,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Solution:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.261,
            0.881,
            0.301
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 1:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Substitute (–3, 4) into the first equation, (that is, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = - 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = 4 )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.386,
            0.299,
            0.585,
            0.376
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{r}3a(-3)+4(4)=-2 \\\\ -9a+16=-2 \\\\ -9a=-18 \\\\ a=2\\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.382,
            0.691,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 2:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Substitute (–3, 4) into the second equation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.306,
            0.406,
            0.579,
            0.485
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array} { r l r } & { } & { 2 ( - 3 ) + b ( 4 ) = 1 4 } \\\\ { \\mathrm { o r } } & { } & { - 6 + 4 b = 1 4 } \\\\ { \\mathrm { o r } } & { } & { 4 b = 2 0 } \\\\ { \\mathrm { o r } } & { } & { b = 5 } \\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.496,
            0.399,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.535,
            0.853,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Four friends— Ranju, Meena, Farhan, and Toshi — are solving problems. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ranju:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " I noticed something! If c = 0 in the standard form of a line "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "ax + by + c = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " then the line must pass through the origin. Look, if I substitute x = 0 and y = 0, in equation ax + by = 0, the equation is satisfied. So, the origin lies on the line! We can also say that the line passes through the origin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.657,
            0.851,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Farhan:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let us try for the equation 2x + 3y = 0. Here a = 2, b = 3 but "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "c = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If we substitute x = 0, then we get 3y = 0 or y = 0. This means (0, 0) lies on the line. So yes, this line passes through the origin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.718,
            0.852,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Toshi:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Suppose our equation has b = c = 0, say, 5x = 0. This becomes x = 0 which is the equation of the y-axis. And the y-axis passes through the origin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.778,
            0.851,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meena:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " And if we take a = c = 0, say, 7y = 0, that means y = 0, which is the equation of the x-axis that also passes through the origin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.82,
            0.852,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, they conclude: Whenever c = 0, the line "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "ax + by + c = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " will always pass through the origin, irrespective of the values of a or b. Do you agree with them?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
